## What the RCE Can and Cannot Touch## 🟢 What It Affects (Inside the Server Machine)
An attacker exploiting this vulnerability gains the ability to interact with anything that the user account running the server process has permission to touch:

* Local Files: They can read, create, or delete files inside your directories (like your note.html or rce_server.js files).
* System Processes: They can look at running applications (ps aux) or view environment keys stored in the server memory.
* Internal Network Sockets: They can see what internal ports are listening on that machine (ss -antp). [4, 5, 6, 7, 8] 

## 🔴 What It CANNOT Touch (Outside the Server Machine)

* The Victim's Computer: If someone visits your vulnerable website from their home computer using a web browser, the RCE cannot access their local files, read their webcam, or touch their operating system. The code is running on your server, not on their laptop. [9, 10, 11, 12] 
* Other Isolated Networks: It cannot automatically touch external third-party servers unless your server machine is specifically configured to route traffic directly to them.

------------------------------
## Why this is different from XSS
This distinction highlights the clear boundary between client-side bugs and server-side takeovers:

* XSS (Client-Side): Affects the website visitors. The malicious JavaScript code runs inside the browser of whoever clicks the link, meaning it targets user sessions and cookies. [13, 14, 15] 
* RCE (Server-Side): Affects the infrastructure. The malicious shell command runs directly on the backend server hardware, meaning it targets the company's application data and local machine files. [16, 17] 

